# Frontend environment.
#
# Copy to `.env` or `.env.local`. **This file is a template and Vite never reads it** —
# editing `.env.example` has no effect on the running app. Vite reads `.env`,
# `.env.local`, `.env.[mode]` and `.env.[mode].local`, and nothing else. Both `.env` and
# `.env.local` are gitignored.
#
# **You usually need nothing here.** The dev server already proxies /api to the deployed
# API, so a fresh clone runs with real data and no setup. The two variables below are
# overrides.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_API_PROXY_TARGET — where the dev server forwards /api
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defaults to https://api.zunopilot.com (see vite.config.ts).
#
# **Set this to your own backend before doing anything that writes.** On the default,
# logging in, sending a message, editing a template or creating an order from a dev
# session acts on live production data.
#
# VITE_API_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_API_BASE_URL — leave this unset
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /api is same-origin in both environments: the dev server proxies it, and in production
# `deploy/nginx/app` proxies `location ^~ /api` to the backend on 127.0.0.1:4000. So the
# relative `/api` fallback in src/lib/api.ts is correct everywhere.
#
# Setting an absolute URL makes the browser call that origin directly, which:
#   * bypasses the dev proxy, so VITE_API_PROXY_TARGET stops applying;
#   * is refused by the browser, because backend/src/app.ts restricts CORS to
#     { FRONTEND_URL, APP_URL, SUPERADMIN_ORIGIN } and localhost:5173 is not among them;
#   * must be https:// if you do it at all — an http:// request from an https:// page is
#     blocked as mixed content.
#
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.zunopilot.com/api
